  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Emergency Shelter and NFI for Affected Populations of the Floods in Northeastern Libya</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">On 10 September 2023, Storm Daniel made landfall in Libya causing severe weather conditions, including strong winds and sudden heavy rainfall affecting the northeastern areas of Benghazi, Tobruk, Toukra, Talmeitha, Almarj, Taknes (Al Jabal Al Akhdar), Al Owailia, B ayada, Albayda, Shahhat, Sousa and Derna. The storm caused two dams on the Wadi Derna River to burst and send millions of cubic meters of water downstream to inundate the river plain, hitting Derna. The International Organization for Migration (IOM) estimates that 37,000 people have been displaced. The human toll is significant and is expected to continue rising; 11,300 are presumed dead, with a total of 3,958 deaths having been registered in hospitals. Rescue teams continue their search for survivors.The proposed intervention responds to urgent shelter and non-food item needs identified by IOM, the United Nations and the Government of Libya in the most affected areas by the floods to provide emergency relief assistance to the most vulnerable. IOM is contributing to efforts to mitigate the impact of the flood, restore dignified living conditions of people affected, and address the deteriorated humanitarian conditions along the northeastern side of Libya.</narrative>
  </description>
